Lecture Embedded Computing - script and slides
===============================================================================

Build
  make            builds docu.pdf (script) and slides.pdf (slides)
  make script     only the script
  make slides     only the slides
  make clean      removes LaTeX build artifacts

Structure

  docu.tex (script, A4)        slides.tex (slides, A5 landscape)
        \                        /
         +---- preamble.tex ----+      common packages, listings setup
         +---- mathmacros.tex --+      macros (\bi, \ite, \defn, \qbox, ...)
         |                      +--    mathmacros-sl.tex (slide overrides)
         +---- text.tex --------+      list of chapters
                  |
   Intro, Modelling, Cyber_Physical_Systems, Processors, Programming,
   Interfaces, tcp_ip_serial, OperatingSystems   (*.tex)

Script vs. slides
  \nsl{...}   text only in the script
  \os{...}    text only on the slides (typically \os{\newpage} = slide break)

Figures live in figures/; the figures of tcp_ip_serial.tex in images/.
Runnable examples live in examples/<chapter>/ (each with its own README.md):
  modelling/    ch. 2  coffee machine FSM, C and Rust (Pico)
  cps/          ch. 3  Modelica models, Python simulation (plots of ch. 3)
  processors/   ch. 4  one C function compiled for AVR, Cortex-M0+/M4, RISC-V
  programming/  ch. 5  assembly from C and Rust, Rust library with tests,
                       MicroPython, Python
  interfaces/   ch. 6  GPIO, IRQ, PWM, ADC, UART, I2C, SPI, watchdog (Pico)
  networking/   ch. 7  Wi-Fi, HTTP, NTP, MQTT (ESP32-C3), sockets on the PC
  os/           ch. 8  FreeRTOS and Embassy tasks, queues, mutex (ESP32-C3)
C examples run in the browser on https://wokwi.com, Rust examples are built
with cargo and simulated with the Wokwi extension for VS Code.

Literature: refs.bib (all entries are listed in script and slides).
All sources are UTF-8 with LF line endings.
